  "text": "At Berkeley, Michael Stonebraker and Eugene Wong built a relational system of their own, called Ingres. Its code went out for the cost of a tape, and its people seeded an industry, all the way to Microsoft SQL Server.\n\nIn eighty-six, Stonebraker started over, on a database you could extend with your own types. He called it Postgres... as in, after Ingres. One catch. It didn't speak SQL, and SQL had won. So in ninety-four, two grad students, Andrew Yu and Jolly Chen, gave it SQL. Hello, Postgres ninety-five. Volunteers kept it going, and in ninety-six renamed it. Officially, post-gres-cue-ell... but most of us just say Postgres.\n\nCompile it to WebAssembly, and you get PGlite, Postgres running right in your browser. It's run every query in this course. Ask it who it is, and it tells you. Postgres eighteen point three. And it kept its upbringing. Raised in a lab and then by volunteers, not a sales team, it says no to rows that break your rules. It's easier to design carefully on a database that takes your rules seriously... and now you know why this one does.",
